<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Spacing and shape tokens — SPEC.md §7.

    One spacing scale, one corner radius, applied everywhere. No size is
    written into a layout.

    The radius is deliberately small: in this interface the ONLY perfectly
    round element is the availability indicator. Everything else is nearly
    straight, which makes the indicator stand out through contrast of shape
    rather than through louder colour. It is also what keeps the application
    away from the stack of heavily rounded cards a default Material theme
    produces.

    The scale is declared in full even though not all its rungs are used yet:
    that is what makes it a system. Picking a value case by case as the need
    arises is exactly what a scale prevents.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools" tools:ignore="UnusedResources">

    <!-- Spacing scale, base 4. -->
    <dimen name="space_xs">4dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_s">8dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_m">12dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_l">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_xl">24dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_xxl">32dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="space_xxxl">48dp</dimen>

    <!-- The single corner radius. -->
    <dimen name="radius">4dp</dimen>
    <!-- The thickness of separator strokes and outlines. -->
    <dimen name="stroke">1dp</dimen>

    <!-- The minimum touch target, imposed by SPEC §7. -->
    <dimen name="touch_target_min">48dp</dimen>

    <!-- The availability indicator: the signature element. Its size is
         calibrated to stay legible at arm's length, while walking.

         The disc is in `sp` and not in `dp`, the one box in this file that
         follows the text scale rather than the screen. It holds a figure and
         nothing else, so a disc frozen in `dp` cut the count off inside its own
         ring for anybody who had asked Android for large text — worst exactly
         where the figure matters most, at a station of more than a hundred
         docks.

         These three numbers are read by `AvailabilityIndicatorView` as the
         **proportions** they set out — 52 to 24 to 16 — and not one by one. From
         Android 14 a size in `sp` grows along a curve rather than by the factor
         asked for, and the larger the declared number the less of the factor it
         receives: measured on a Fairphone 3 at ×2.0, the 16 comes out at ×1.75,
         the 24 at ×1.50 and the 52 at ×1.11. Read separately the three tokens
         therefore drift apart, and the drawing they describe stops being the
         drawing. Read as proportions of the body text, they hold at every
         setting, whatever the curve does now or later. At the default scale
         nothing moves for anybody who has asked for nothing.

         The ring keeps its `dp`: it is a stroke and not a letter, and it only
         reads finer as the disc grows. -->
    <dimen name="indicator_size">52sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="indicator_ring">4dp</dimen>

    <!-- The shape of a journey: discs, strokes and their dashes. The disc is
         the marker of the journey map, at the size a row of four holds on the
         narrowest screen. -->
    <dimen name="journey_shape_node">32dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="journey_shape_walk_stroke">2dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="journey_shape_ride_stroke">3dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="journey_shape_dash">2dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="journey_shape_dash_gap">5dp</dimen>
    <!-- How far a label stands off the disc it is level with. Measured from the
         disc, but read against the stroke it labels, which runs half a disc
         further in: a gap that looks tight here still leaves the label a
         comfortable distance from its own line, and a roomy one had the figures
         floating clear of the drawing they belong to. -->
    <dimen name="journey_shape_label_gap">2dp</dimen>

    <!-- The ground the ride runs over, drawn on the journey's detail. Tall
         enough for a climb halfway along to be told apart from one at the end,
         short enough not to push the stations off the screen. -->
    <dimen name="journey_profile_height">96dp</dimen>

    <!-- The marker heading a place on the journey's detail: the map's own
         marker, at the size the drawing above it uses for the same disc. -->
    <dimen name="journey_place_marker">32dp</dimen>

    <!-- The bike that crosses the screen while a journey is worked out. Large
         on purpose: it is alone on its screen and has no neighbour to be
         proportionate to, unlike the markers it borrows its drawing from. -->
    <dimen name="computing_bike_size">96dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="computing_bike_lane_gap">80dp</dimen>

    <!-- The opening screen (SPEC §7.0).

         The canvas is not a size chosen here: it is the box Android's own
         splash screen draws an icon in, 288dp, and matching it is the whole
         point. The launcher icon's drawing occupies the middle 60dp out of its
         108dp canvas, so at 288dp the bicycle reads at 160dp — exactly what
         the system was showing a frame earlier. Any other value makes the mark
         jump the moment the application takes over.

         The name hangs from the middle of the window, which is where the mark
         is centred: half the bicycle as drawn, 80dp, plus one rung of the
         spacing scale. Measuring from the middle rather than from the image's
         edge is what skips the canvas's transparent margin, 64dp of nothing
         that would otherwise push the name away from the drawing. -->
    <dimen name="intro_mark_canvas">288dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="intro_name_drop">104dp</dimen>

    <!-- The station marks of the welcome screen's fleet page (SPEC §7.9).

         Nearly twice the 36dp the map gives the same disc, and the reason is
         what the page is for. On the map the disc answers "is there a station
         here" among fifty others, and the bike inside it is a silhouette. Here
         it answers "which of the three is this", and what has to be told apart
         is the cog and the bolt on the bike's flanks — the cog is drawn with a
         radius of 3.90 in a 24-unit glyph riding at 0.7 of the disc, so it
         spans a little over five units of thirty-six: 5dp at the map's size,
         a dot, against some 10dp here, about the height of a lowercase letter
         of the sentence it faces. Larger still would crowd that sentence,
         which is what carries the meaning. -->
    <dimen name="welcome_mark">64dp</dimen>

    <!-- Typographic scale. -->
    <dimen name="text_title">22sp</dimen>
    <!-- The name on the opening screen, and the one rung of the scale that
         belongs to a single screen. It earns the exception: everywhere else a
         title shares its screen with the text it heads, whereas this one has
         a whole green field to itself and a 160dp mark above it, against which
         the title size read as a caption. -->
    <dimen name="text_intro">32sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="text_indicator">24sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="text_body">16sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="text_secondary">14sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="text_label">12sp</dimen>

</resources>
